The Security Council,

Recalling all its relevant resolutions on the situation in the Middle East, including the Palestinian question,

Guided by the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Unequivocally rejecting and condemning in the strongest terms the heinous terrorist attacks by Hamas and other terrorist groups that took place in Israel starting on 7 October 2023, the taking and holding of hostages, the murder, intentional killing, and maiming of civilians, and sexual violence, and demanding the immediate and unconditional release of all remaining hostages,

Reaffirming the inherent right of all States to individual and collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter,

Emphasizing that all parties to conflicts must fully comply with their obligations under international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law, notably regarding the protection of civilians and civilian infrastructure, and the rules of distinction, proportionality, and precaution,

Expressing deep concern over the rapidly deteriorating humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip and its severe impact on the civilian population, largely comprising children and women, and stressing the urgent need for expanded, sustained, safe, and unhindered humanitarian access,

Reiterating that civilians in Gaza must be protected, that civilian infrastructure, including facilities belonging to the United Nations and humanitarian personnel, must be respected and protected, and that essential services and goods indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, including water, electricity, fuel, food, and medical supplies, must be urgently facilitated,

Noting the ongoing diplomatic efforts by regional and international actors, including the United States, Egypt, and the United Nations, to secure the release of hostages and to enable the entry of essential humanitarian assistance through the Rafah crossing,

Warning against any spillover of the conflict into the wider region, and expressing grave concern at the escalation of tensions and exchanges of fire across the Blue Line and throughout the region,

Reaffirming its unwavering commitment to the vision of the two-State solution where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders,

1. Unequivocally condemns all terrorist attacks by Hamas and other terrorist groups in Israel since 7 October 2023, as well as the taking and holding of hostages, and demands the immediate, unconditional, and safe release of all hostages held in Gaza;

2. Reaffirms the inherent right of all States to individual and collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations, and underlines that in responding to terrorist attacks, Member States must comply with all their obligations under international law, in particular international humanitarian law;

3. Calls for all measures, including humanitarian pauses, to allow the full, rapid, safe, and unhindered humanitarian access for United Nations humanitarian agencies and their implementing partners, the International Committee of the Red Cross, and other humanitarian organizations, to provide essential goods and services to civilians in need throughout the Gaza Strip;

4. Urges all parties to respect and protect civilian objects and critical civilian infrastructure, including hospitals, medical facilities, schools, places of worship, and United Nations facilities, as well as humanitarian and medical personnel;

5. Emphasizes that civilians must not be forcibly displaced and that any displacement must be temporary, voluntary, and accompanied by proper accommodations ensuring safety, hygiene, and access to necessary sustenance, with the right to return home as soon as hostilities cease;

6. Calls on all States to take practical steps to prevent the export of arms, materiel, and financial support to armed militias and terrorist groups operating in Gaza, including Hamas;

7. Condemns all attacks by Hezbollah and other armed groups against Israel in violation of relevant Security Council resolutions, and calls on all regional actors to cease all actions that could exacerbate the crisis or broaden the conflict;

8. Welcomes the initial delivery of humanitarian aid to Gaza through the Rafah crossing, commends the efforts of Egypt, the United Nations, and regional partners, and calls for the continuous, unimpeded, and scaled-up flow of humanitarian assistance;

9. Reaffirms the need to redouble international efforts to achieve a comprehensive, just, and lasting peace based on the two-State solution, consistent with international law and relevant United Nations resolutions;

10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

